      "text": "To determine by how many square inches the area of the circle is greater than the area of the square, we need to calculate the areas of both the square and the circle and then find the difference between these areas.\n\nFirst, let's calculate the area of the square. The diagonal of the square is given as 5 inches. Let the side length of the square be \\( s \\). The relationship between the side length and the diagonal of a square is given by the Pythagorean theorem:\n\\[\ns\\sqrt{2} = 5\n\\]\nSolving for \\( s \\), we get:\n\\[\ns = \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}} = \\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2}\n\\]\nThe area \\( A_{\\text{square}} \\) of the square is:\n\\[\nA_{\\text{square}} = s^2 = \\left(\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)^2 = \\frac{25 \\cdot 2}{4} = \\frac{50}{4} = 12.5 \\text{ square inches}\n\\]\n\nNext, let's calculate the area of the circle. The diameter of the circle is given as 5 inches, so the radius \\( r \\) is:\n\\[\nr = \\frac{5}{2} = 2.5 \\text{ inches}\n\\]\nThe area \\( A_{\\text{circle}} \\) of the circle is:\n\\[\nA_{\\text{circle}} = \\pi r^2 = \\pi (2.5)^2 = \\pi \\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)^2 = \\pi \\left(\\frac{25}{4}\\right) = \\frac{25\\pi}{4} \\text{ square inches}\n\\]\n\nTo find the difference between the area of the circle and the area of the square, we calculate:\n\\[\nA_{\\text{circle}} - A_{\\text{square}} = \\frac{25\\pi}{4} - 12.5 = \\frac{25\\pi}{4} - \\frac{50}{4} = \\frac{25\\pi - 50}{4}\n\\]\nUsing the approximation \\(\\pi \\approx 3.14159\\), we get:\n\\[\n25\\pi - 50 \\approx 25 \\times 3.14159 - 50 = 78.53975 - 50 = 28.53975\n\\]\nThus,\n\\[\n\\frac{25\\pi - 50}{4} \\approx \\frac{28.53975}{4} \\approx 7.13\n\\]\nTherefore, the area of the circle is greater than the area of the square by approximately \\(\\boxed{7.1}\\) square inches.",
