      "text": "To determine by how many square inches the area of the circle is greater than the area of the square, we need to calculate the areas of both the square and the circle and then find the difference between them.\n\nFirst, let's calculate the area of the square. The diagonal of the square is given as 5 inches. If we let the side length of the square be \\( s \\), then the diagonal \\( d \\) of a square with side length \\( s \\) can be found using the Pythagorean theorem:\n\\[ d = s\\sqrt{2} \\]\nGiven \\( d = 5 \\), we have:\n\\[ s\\sqrt{2} = 5 \\]\nSolving for \\( s \\):\n\\[ s = \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}} = \\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\]\nThe area \\( A_{\\text{square}} \\) of the square is:\n\\[ A_{\\text{square}} = s^2 = \\left( \\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\right)^2 = \\frac{25 \\cdot 2}{4} = \\frac{50}{4} = 12.5 \\text{ square inches} \\]\n\nNext, let's calculate the area of the circle. The diameter of the circle is given as 5 inches, so the radius \\( r \\) is:\n\\[ r = \\frac{5}{2} = 2.5 \\text{ inches} \\]\nThe area \\( A_{\\text{circle}} \\) of the circle is:\n\\[ A_{\\text{circle}} = \\pi r^2 = \\pi (2.5)^2 = \\pi \\cdot 6.25 = 6.25\\pi \\text{ square inches} \\]\n\nNow, we need to find the difference between the area of the circle and the area of the square:\n\\[ \\text{Difference} = A_{\\text{circle}} - A_{\\text{square}} = 6.25\\pi - 12.5 \\]\nUsing the approximation \\( \\pi \\approx 3.14159 \\), we get:\n\\[ 6.25\\pi \\approx 6.25 \\cdot 3.14159 = 19.6349375 \\]\nSo,\n\\[ \\text{Difference} \\approx 19.6349375 - 12.5 = 7.1349375 \\]\nRounding to the nearest tenth, we get:\n\\[ \\text{Difference} \\approx 7.1 \\]\n\nTherefore, the area of the circle is greater than the area of the square by \\(\\boxed{7.1}\\) square inches.",
